          'Driving an RL or RC circuit with a sinusoid and solving the differential equation directly produces the right answer but demands laborious trigonometric collection of sine and cosine terms. The complex forcing function replaces the real drive V_m·cos(ωt) with V_m·e^(j(ωt+φ)), solves the now far simpler equation, and takes the real part at the end. This is legitimate because the circuit is linear: a complex source is just a real source plus j times a second real source in quadrature, and superposition guarantees the real part of the response belongs to the real part of the drive. Every e^(jωt) factor then appears in every term and cancels, which is what leaves behind the purely algebraic phasor equation.',
