        definition:
          'Maximum power is delivered to a load when the load resistance equals the Thévenin resistance of the source network. The derivation is a single differentiation: p = V_th²·R_L/(R_th + R_L)², and setting dp/dR_L = 0 gives R_L = R_th, at which point p_max = V_th²/(4R_th). Two consequences are routinely missed. First, the optimum is flat — a load mismatched by a factor of two still delivers about 89 % of the maximum, which is why exact matching is rarely worth expensive components. Second, at the match exactly half the total power is dissipated inside the source, so efficiency is 50 %, and any design that values efficiency over delivered power must deliberately mismatch.',
