        definition:
          'Convolution is the time-domain operation corresponding to multiplication in the s-domain: if Y(s) = H(s)·X(s) then y(t) = ∫₀^t h(τ)·x(t − τ) dτ. Physically it says the output at any instant is the accumulated effect of every past input sample, each weighted by how much of its impulse response survives to the present — which is why the impulse response fully characterises a linear time-invariant circuit. In practice the s-domain route is almost always easier, and convolution is used when the impulse response is known only as measured data rather than as a formula, or when the input is not analytically transformable.',
        takeaway: 'Convolution is what multiplication in s means physically — the present output is a weighted memory of past inputs.',
        visual: 'convolution-flip-slide',
        visualSpec:
          'A three-track animation panel. Track 1 shows the input x(τ) as a rectangular pulse fixed in place. Track 2 shows h(t − τ), the impulse response flipped left-right and sliding rightward as t increases, drawn in a second colour. Track 3 shows the growing output y(t), with the current value drawn as a filled dot. At each slide position, the overlap area between tracks 1 and 2 is shaded and an arrow carries that area down to plot the corresponding point on track 3. A side note reads "Y(s) = H(s)·X(s)". On reveal: track 1 draws, track 2 appears flipped at the far left, then slides right in steps with the overlap shading and the arrow plotting a new point on track 3 at each step, then the side note appears once the output curve is complete.',
