        definition:
          'A pole position on the s-plane encodes both the transient and the frequency behaviour of a circuit, and the first-order low-pass filter is the clearest demonstration. Its single pole at s = −1/RC sits on the negative real axis; the distance from the origin is simultaneously the reciprocal of the time constant and the −3 dB cutoff in radians per second. Moving the pole further from the origin widens the bandwidth and shortens the settling time in exactly the same proportion, so the product of bandwidth and rise time is a constant — approximately 0.35 when bandwidth is in hertz and rise time in seconds. This is the reason a fast circuit must be a wideband circuit, and why one cannot be improved without the other.',
