        slides: [47, 48, 49],
        terms: ['natural frequencies', 'left half plane', 'stability', 'jω axis poles', 'damping from pole angle'],
        definition:
          'The poles of a network function are its natural frequencies, and their positions on the s-plane classify the natural response completely. A pole on the negative real axis gives a pure decaying exponential whose rate is the distance from the origin. A complex conjugate pair in the left half plane gives a damped oscillation whose ringing frequency is the imaginary part and whose envelope decay is the real part; the angle from the negative real axis therefore sets the damping ratio. A pole on the jω axis gives an undamped oscillation and a pole in the right half plane gives exponential growth, so a passive network — which can never be unstable — always has its poles in the closed left half plane.',
        takeaway: 'Real part sets how fast it dies, imaginary part sets how fast it rings, and anything not in the left half plane does not settle.',
        visual: 's-plane-response-atlas',
        visualSpec:
          'A large s-plane divided into regions, each carrying a small inset time-response sketch pinned at a representative pole location. Far left on the real axis: a fast-decaying exponential. Nearer the origin on the real axis: a slow exponential. Upper-left quadrant with its conjugate below: a damped ringing waveform with its envelope. On the jω axis: a constant-amplitude sinusoid, marked amber. Right half plane: a growing exponential, marked red with a "not passive" tag. Dotted radial lines from the origin are labelled with damping ratios. On reveal: axes draw, then each region\'s pole marker and its inset sketch appear in order from far-left to right-half-plane, with the amber and red tags landing last, then the radial damping-ratio lines sweep in.',
