          'A one-port is any network accessed through a single terminal pair, and it is described completely by one function — its driving-point impedance Z(s) = V(s)/I(s). This is the Thévenin idea of Module 2 generalised into the s-domain: whatever the internal complexity, the outside world sees only that one ratio. The defining requirement is the port condition: the current entering one terminal must equal the current leaving the other. If a stray path lets current return by another route, the terminal pair is not a port and no single impedance describes it. The driving-point impedance of a passive network is always a positive real function, which is the realisability constraint met at the end of Module 4.',
