          'A two-port has an input terminal pair and an output terminal pair, each satisfying the port condition, and it is described by relationships among four variables: V₁, I₁, V₂ and I₂. Because the network is linear, any two of these can be expressed as linear combinations of the other two, which takes exactly four coefficients — and that is why a two-port is a four-number object regardless of how many elements it contains internally. This is a genuine compression: a filter with thirty components and a filter with three are equally described by four numbers at a given frequency. The description is valid only for what happens at the ports, and says nothing about internal node voltages or internal dissipation.',
